menu "WiFi Dap web server (HTTP / WebSocket)"

    # ------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Slim-build feature switches (2026-10-26).  All default n: the code is
    # kept in the tree and can be re-enabled from the full-features backup
    # (tag backup/full-features-2026-10-26, or the matching Kconfig options).
    # Per-item macro / file / restore recipe / resource cost:
    # docs/feature-flags.md.
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------

    config BIRDIEBOT_WEB_OTA_UPLOAD_ENABLE
        bool "Enable the Web OTA upload endpoint (POST /api/ota/upload)"
        default n
        help
            Slim build (A1): removes the browser OTA upload feature.  When
            disabled, uri_modules/uri_ota.c compiles to a read-only
            diagnostics stub (the frozen /api/target/status ota_upload_*
            field set stays present and reports inactive), so the URI
            handler, the upload worker task and its static 2 x 4 KiB receive
            buffers are gone.

            OTA partitions, the bootloader rollback configuration and
            wt_ota_mark_app_valid() are kept for every target so images can
            still be installed through a future/recovery path
            (docs/feature-flags.md A1/A2).

    config BIRDIEBOT_WEB_DISCOVER_ENABLE
        bool "Enable GET /api/discover (nearby DAP discovery via mDNS)"
        default n
        depends on BIRDIEBOT_TARGET_FLASH_ENABLE
        help
            Slim build (H3): removes the nearby-device discovery endpoint.
            When disabled, the /api/discover URI, its JSON row buffer and the
            blocking mDNS _http._tcp query (components/target_flash
            tf_discover.c) are compiled out; the Web keeps the manual /
            recent-device list and treats the missing endpoint as
            "no discovery".  mDNS advertising of this device (wt_mdns) is
            NOT affected.

    config BIRDIEBOT_WEB_HOST_GUARD
        bool "Enable the browser Host/Origin guard (CSWSH / DNS rebinding)"
        default n
        help
            SECURITY RELEVANT - disabled by default in the slim build by
            explicit project decision (2026-10-26, docs/feature-flags.md M1
            and docs/security.md).  The implementation stays in
            web_host_guard.c and every handler keeps its call site, so
            re-enabling is one Kconfig line.

            With the guard disabled, HTTP and WebSocket handlers accept any
            Host/Origin value:
              * a malicious web page on the same network can open
                ws://<device-ip>/ws from another origin (cross-site
                WebSocket hijacking, CSWSH) and drive the DAP/target;
              * a DNS-rebinding hostname resolves to the device and a
                browser can operate it as same-origin.
            This is only acceptable on a trusted lab/office LAN because the
            firmware has no login/auth layer at all (docs/security.md).

    config BIRDIEBOT_TARGET_FLASH_ENABLE
        bool "Enable Web target-flash endpoints (/api/target/*)"
        default y
        help
            Registers the browser-driven direct-flash HTTP endpoints:

              GET  /api/target/info     probe the SWD target, return identity
                                        (family/DPIDR/CPUID/IDCODE/UID/flash)
              POST /api/target/flash    octet-stream upload; the body is
                                        streamed over SWD and programmed into the
                                        target flash while it arrives (no
                                        staging partition)
              GET  /api/target/status   flash engine status snapshot
              POST /api/target/reset    optional {"run":true} SWD reset/run

            The flash algorithm lives in components/target_flash (CMSIS-DAP
            over the existing SWD pins).  While flashing, the flash engine owns
            the DAP exclusively: USBIP / WS-DAP / elaphureLink sessions are
            paused and restored afterwards.  The browser-side upload progress
            is driven by XHR events; other HTTP requests are only serviced
            between chunks and during the erase/verify phases.

            Intended for an internal/lab LAN only (the web login feature was
            removed by project decision; the browser Host/Origin guard can be
            enabled with CONFIG_BIRDIEBOT_WEB_HOST_GUARD).  Disable to compile
            the endpoints out.

            The separate GET /api/discover endpoint is registered together
            with these URIs and has its own switch above.

    config BIRDIEBOT_WEB_ALLOWED_ORIGIN
        string "Extra allowed Host/Origin entries (comma separated)"
        default ""
        depends on BIRDIEBOT_WEB_HOST_GUARD
        help
            Comma-separated allow-list for the browser Host/Origin check in
            web_host_guard.c (CSWSH / DNS-rebinding hardening; this is a
            browser-safety policy, not a login feature).  Only used when
            CONFIG_BIRDIEBOT_WEB_HOST_GUARD=y.  Each entry is either a full
            origin (scheme://host[:port]) or a bare hostname, e.g.
                "http://192.168.1.1:8080,https://dap.local"

            - Host header check: the request must carry a Host that is an IP
              literal, an entry from this list, or CONFIG_LWIP_LOCAL_HOSTNAME.
              This blocks DNS-rebinding hostnames (an attacker-controlled DNS
              name resolving to the device IP is rejected).
            - Origin header check: when a browser sends Origin it must be
              same-origin with the request Host, or match an entry from this
              list (blocks cross-site WebSocket hijacking, CSWSH).

            Accessing the device through its IP (the documented way,
            docs/protocol.md §1) needs no entry.  Accessing it through a
            hostname (e.g. the mDNS name "dap.local", or a reverse-proxy
            name) requires adding that name here, e.g.
                CONFIG_BIRDIEBOT_WEB_ALLOWED_ORIGIN="http://dap.local"

endmenu
